    "slot_key": "Entity-to-entity linkage rules",
    "slot_value": "Connect related entities and events; preserve the core public health workflow so staff can review a signal, assign investigation ownership, document case and contact details, and record actions taken, while centralizing and making more consistent the linking of entities; treat lab reports, case reports, and referrals as related but distinct sources, and allow multiple sources to attach to the same investigation without losing original details. Use a single shared case record with linked entities underneath it so everyone works from the same source of truth; attribute each person's actions, notes, and status changes to their user account and timestamp them; prevent duplicate contacts or exposures by matching on identifiers and let staff merge records when needed; keep conflicting updates in the audit trail and require an authorized reviewer to resolve them before reports are finalized. The shared case should hold the core investigation status and overall summary, while linked people, contacts, exposures, and actions can be edited separately when they belong to different parts of the investigation; editing should be limited to what the investigator's role and assignment allow; linked details such as identifying information, lab results, and sensitive interview notes may need to remain restricted to a smaller group for legal or public health reasons; if a linked record is used in multiple active workflows, edits should be controlled carefully to prevent one team from overwriting information another team still depends on. Anything already committed to the database should not be rolled back just because a later save timed out; preserve unsaved changes, but if the underlying record was already submitted or linked by someone else, conflicting edits should be cleared or flagged for review before resubmitting; sensitive or system-generated fields should be refreshed from the saved record rather than restored from the user's old screen state.",
