[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-6-2-3",
    "slot_key": "Audit and disclosure restrictions",
    "slot_value": "Staff should be able to see the previous status, who made the change, when it happened, and a short reason or note explaining why. For merged or reclassified records, preserve the chain of what happened so investigators can understand the decision later. History should not be hidden, but it may be summarized for everyday users while remaining available in full to authorized staff. On conflicting batch imports or external feeds, the system should block automatic updates, preserve both versions for review, display conflicting fields side by side, indicate any more authoritative source if defined, and retain the original data for audit purposes even if a preference rule is allowed for clearly minor, one-sided conflicts. The system should keep both conflicting versions in the audit trail and require an authorized reviewer to resolve the conflict before reports are finalized. Offline access should be limited to only the cases and data the investigator already has permission to see, with sensitive fields tightly restricted. Investigators may draft notes and queue certain updates while offline, but anything that could overwrite someone else's work must wait for sync and conflict checking after reconnecting. If the case changed while they were away, the system should preserve both the offline submission and the newer online version for reviewer reconciliation rather than silently replacing one with the other. Anything that has already been committed to the database should not be rolled back just because a later save timed out; if the underlying record was already submitted or linked by someone else, conflicting edits should be cleared or flagged for review before resubmission, and sensitive or system-generated fields should be refreshed from the saved record rather than restored from the user's old screen state. Authoritative identity, status, timestamp, linkage, ownership, and audit-related values should be reloaded from the persisted record after a save timeout, including case status, assigned investigator, last updated time, linked contacts or labs, and system-set identifiers or flags; any staged change in that category should only replace the saved value after explicit user confirmation. Pending edits should be preserved, with out-of-date or conflicting parts selectively flagged for review; purely local notes or draft comments can stay as-is, while edits to shared fields like demographics, status, relationships, exposures, or specimen details should be highlighted for reconciliation.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

