    "slot_key": "Urgent change approval and rollback path",
    "slot_value": "Urgent changes should be approved by a small set of authorized supervisors or system admins, with the approval captured in the audit trail. The change should take effect quickly, but the system should keep the prior version available so it can be rolled back right away if it disrupts active work or turns out to be wrong. If reversed, staff should be able to restore the previous state without losing the history of what happened or who approved it. When a linked person, contact, exposure, or action record is shared across more than one active workflow, the person actively assigned or checked out for an urgent task gets edit priority first; everyone else should see it read-only or work on a draft. If the record is high-risk for conflicting changes, it should be briefly locked or require supervisor approval before commit. If updates still collide, the system should keep both, flag the conflict immediately, and have a reviewer decide which version becomes operative. A shared record should be locked or require supervisor approval when the change affects case status, exposure classification, contact tracing assignments, or anything that could change who is notified or what public health action happens next; approval is also expected when the record is already being edited by someone else or when the user is trying to change restricted sensitive data. A time-critical containment update, such as a positive lab result or urgent isolation action, can bypass the lock if a qualified supervisor or designated responder forces the update, but the system must log exactly why and alert the other users. Anything that changes the operational picture in a meaningful way should update immediately, especially if it affects case status, contact tracing, or an active investigation queue; new lab results or any record state change that requires action should refresh right away or at least prompt a near-real-time update.",
