    "slot_number": "slot-7-1-dyn-11",
    "slot_key": "Offline access and sync for key investigative tasks",
    "slot_value": "Investigators should be able to access only the cases and data they already have permission to see while offline, with sensitive fields tightly restricted. They can draft notes and queue certain updates offline, but anything that could overwrite someone else’s work must wait for sync and conflict checking when they reconnect. If the case changed while they were away, the system should preserve both the offline submission and the newer online version for reviewer reconciliation instead of silently replacing one with the other. Anything authoritative about identity, status, timestamps, linkage, ownership, or audit-related values should be reloaded from the persisted record after a save timeout, including case status, assigned investigator, last updated time, linked contacts or labs, and any system-set identifiers or flags. Deliberately staged changes may remain tied to the in-progress entry only until the user explicitly confirms them before they replace the saved value. During recovery, read-only access to existing case details would also be very helpful so people can keep working from what’s already known. Before an incident starts, the core case, contact, exposure, and reporting workflows should be proven and ready, with access controls and backup procedures in place. Immediately after an incident, offline entries should be reconciled, the data validated, and a quick status review completed so nothing was missed.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-7-1-dyn-21",
    "slot_key": "System uptime and incident availability expectation",
    "slot_value": "During an active incident, the system should be very high availability, basically always on, so investigators can rely on it throughout the operational period. Maintenance or downtime should be avoided during peak response if at all possible, and if guaranteed uptime cannot be provided, there must be a clearly defined fallback and notice process. If an incident is still active, planned maintenance should generally be deferred until the operational risk is lower, or performed in a way that does not interrupt investigator access. If downtime is unavoidable, users should get advance notice, and there should be a clear fallback method for capturing critical updates so work isn’t lost. The system should be kept available whenever possible and only taken down when there is a strong reason and a communicated workaround. During the incident, the expectation shifts to stability, quick updates, and visibility into active work.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

