[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-1-dyn-12",
    "slot_key": "Audit trail and prior history visibility for state changes",
    "slot_value": "Staff need to see the previous status, who made the change, when it happened, and a short reason or note explaining why. For merged or reclassified records, the chain of what happened should be preserved so investigators can understand the decision later. The history should not be hidden, though it may be summarized for everyday users while remaining available in full to authorized staff. Each person’s actions, notes, and status changes should be attributed to their user account and timestamped. If conflicting updates occur, the system should keep both versions in the audit trail and require an authorized reviewer to resolve the conflict before reports are finalized. For offline or paper-backed field work, the later system entry time should be shown in the audit trail even when the business record keeps the original action time.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-1-dyn-10",
    "slot_key": "Offline access and sync for field work",
    "slot_value": "Investigators should be able to access only the cases and data they already have permission to see when offline, with sensitive fields tightly restricted. They can draft notes and queue certain updates while offline, but anything that could overwrite someone else’s work must wait for sync and conflict checking when they reconnect. If the case changed while they were away, the system should preserve both the offline submission and the newer online version for reviewer reconciliation instead of silently replacing one with the other. During downtime or recovery, staff also need a way to capture new cases, contacts, exposures, and critical actions through temporary offline logging or paper/emergency forms, then cleanly hand those entries back into the system later without losing timestamps, ownership, or links between records; read-only access to existing case details would also be helpful during recovery. For field work entered later, the business record should keep the original action time while the entry time is stored separately and visible in the audit trail.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

